{
  "dom_id": "questionContainer",
  "name": "Question 35",
  "problem": "Consider the arc of the curve $x=2y+1$ that lies between the lines $y=0$ and $y=1.$ Calculate the surface area $S$ generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$S=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${y}_{1}=0$ to ${y}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2y+1)=2\\,\u21d2\\,{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\sqrt{1+4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi \\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi \\sqrt{5}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\pi \\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
